# Build outputs, ignored for every workload below this directory.
#
# configs.json is emitted by main_bingo.py (--configs_out) as one of the three products of a
# single make rule, alongside the two generated headers; `make clean-data` deletes it. The
# per-workload .gitignore files already cover the headers with `*.h`, but nothing covered the
# JSON, so four of the thirty-seven workloads that emit one had it committed by accident.
#
# Tracking a generated file here is not cosmetic. A build rewrites it whenever params.hjson
# changes, so a sweep leaves the tree dirty and the committed copy silently disagrees with the
# params it was generated from; a `make clean-data` shows up as deleted tracked files.
configs.json

# THE BRING-UP LADDER'S RUNGS ARE LOCAL SCAFFOLDING, not deliverables.
#
# llm_s1_norm .. llm_s5_ffn are prefixes of the same builder (util/sim/llm/
# llm_layer_stages.py): each emits the first N stages of the layer and checks each one, so
# the first rung that fails on hardware names the stage rather than the layer. They exist
# to bisect a bring-up, and they are regenerated from the builder whenever it changes --
# tracking them means every change to the builder shows up as five workloads' worth of
# churn in the diff, and a stale committed rung silently disagrees with the builder it
# came from.
#
# The same goes for the decomposition experiments (_shard, _tp, _t64, _perf): they are
# A/B arms for one question each, not apps anyone ships.
#
# WHAT STAYS TRACKED: llm_layer_4cluster, which is the layer as a standalone app and does
# not go through the ladder at all, and llm_kernels_4cluster, which checks each fused
# kernel against its own golden.
llm_s[0-9]*_*/
llm_layer_shard_4cluster/
llm_layer_tp_4cluster/
llm_layer_t64_4cluster/
llm_layer_perf_4cluster/
